---
// Page body converted from the Claude Design handoff (services-r3.html). Edit freely.
import { site, u } from '../../data/site';
import FormMeta from '../FormMeta.astro';
---
<div class="hero on-tan hero-c">
<div class="hero-in c-center">
<p class="eyebrow"><span class="mark"></span>What we do</p>
<h1 class="h1 hero-h">Everything from bookkeeping to CFO work, run by someone who has <span class="em">done the job himself</span>.</h1>
<p class="lead hero-lead">Six services, one phone number. Take one thing off your plate or hand over the whole back office. Either way it is Kevin doing the work, not a rotating cast of juniors.</p>
<div class="hero-act">
<a class="tel-lg" href={site.tel}>{site.phone}</a>
<a class="btn btn-1" href={u('/contact/')}>Book a call</a>
<a class="btn-txt" href="#included">See what is included</a>
</div>
</div>
</div>

<section style="padding:var(--pad-major) 0">
<div class="seg-in">
<div class="split">
<div>
<p class="eyebrow"><span class="mark"></span>Why us</p>
<h2 class="h2">You get one accountant who already knows what a holdback is, not a portal full of strangers.</h2>
</div>
<div>
<p class="p">Most bookkeeping services are built for a bakery, not a job site. Job costing and retainage are the daily work, and a bookkeeper who has not seen a change order leaves you to explain it.</p>
<p class="p">That is the difference between an app with a chat button, or a big firm that hands you to whoever is free that week, and one CPA, CMA, who already speaks the trade.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div class="pts">
<div class="pt"><span class="pt-n">01</span><h3 class="h4">One person, every month.</h3><p>Kevin does the work himself, so nothing gets lost in a handoff to whoever is on shift.</p></div>
<div class="pt"><span class="pt-n">02</span><h3 class="h4">Built around job costing.</h3><p>WIP, progress billing and holdbacks are the starting point, not something we learn on your file.</p></div>
<div class="pt"><span class="pt-n">03</span><h3 class="h4">A CPA who has worn the boots.</h3><p>Kevin has worked as a subcontractor and for them, so the questions on the first call are the right ones.</p></div>
</div>
</div>
</section>

<div class="cta on-dark cta-c">
<div class="cta-in">
<div>
<p class="eyebrow"><span class="mark"></span>How it starts</p>
<h2 class="h2 cta-h">A scope and a price, in writing, before anything moves.</h2>
<p class="cta-p">Tell Kevin what is eating your week. He will tell you what it costs and exactly what is included, in writing, before you sign anything.</p>
</div>
<div class="cta-act">
<a class="tel" href={site.tel}>{site.phone}</a>
<a class="btn btn-1" href={u('/contact/')}>Book a call</a>
</div>
</div>
</div>

<section id="included" class="on-tan" style="padding:var(--pad-major) 0">
<div class="seg-in c-center">
<p class="eyebrow"><span class="mark"></span>What is included</p>
<h2 class="h2" style="margin-bottom:var(--space-5)">Six things off your plate, and what each one actually covers.</h2>
<p class="p">Take one service or all six. The price only reflects what you hand off, so here is what each one covers in plain terms, past the marketing version.</p>
<ul class="outs">
<li class="out">A close that lands the same day every month, because reconciliation happens weekly, not in a scramble.</li>
<li class="out">Bills paid on schedule, without you approving the same invoice twice.</li>
<li class="out">Invoices that get chased without you making the call at nine at night.</li>
<li class="out">A number for the next quote, because the job costing is already done.</li>
</ul>
<div class="svcs">
<div class="svc-card" id="bookkeeping">
<div><span class="svc-n">01</span><h3 class="h3">Bookkeeping</h3></div>
<div>
<p class="p">This is the monthly work. Every transaction gets coded, the bank and card feeds get reconciled, and the close lands on the same day every month, not whenever someone gets around to it.</p>
<p class="p">Slow closes rarely start in the books. They start on site, in unphotographed receipts. Month one means getting them in before they pile up.</p>
<ul class="list-u">
<li>A set of books you can read without a translator.</li>
<li>Reports that answer whether you can afford the truck before you ask.</li>
</ul>
</div>
</div>
<div class="svc-card" id="accounts-payable">
<div><span class="svc-n">02</span><h3 class="h3">Accounts payable</h3></div>
<div>
<p class="p">Bills get paid on the schedule you set, not the schedule your inbox sets. Every invoice is coded to the right job, checked against what actually arrived, and queued for your approval once, not twice.</p>
<p class="p" style="margin-bottom:0">You keep control of what goes out. You just stop being the one who has to remember which supplier is due on Friday.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div class="svc-card" id="accounts-receivable">
<div><span class="svc-n">03</span><h3 class="h3">Accounts receivable</h3></div>
<div>
<p class="p">Someone has to call the client sitting on an invoice, and it does not have to be you. Follow ups go out on a schedule, so money owed does not quietly become money written off.</p>
<p class="p" style="margin-bottom:0">It is the call most owners put off until it gets awkward. Here it happens before it gets there.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div class="svc-card" id="payroll">
<div><span class="svc-n">04</span><h3 class="h3">Payroll</h3></div>
<div>
<p class="p">The crew gets paid on time, every time, and the deductions go where they are supposed to go. Hours come in from site, pay runs go out on the schedule you set, and the remittances are sent before anyone has to chase them.</p>
<p class="p" style="margin-bottom:0">Wages are coded to the job they were worked on, so labour shows up in your job costing instead of in one lump at month end.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div class="svc-card" id="controllership">
<div><span class="svc-n">05</span><h3 class="h3">Controllership</h3></div>
<div>
<p class="p">Someone senior has to own the numbers, not just enter them. This is the review layer over the books: the month-end close checked before you see it, the accounts that should tie actually tying, and statements you can hand to a bank or a bonding company without a second look.</p>
<p class="p" style="margin-bottom:0">It is the work a full-time controller would do, at the hours a firm this size actually needs.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div class="svc-card" id="fractional-cfo">
<div><span class="svc-n">06</span><h3 class="h3">Fractional CFO</h3></div>
<div>
<p class="p">Past a certain size, the questions change. Not whether this is coded right, but whether you should take this job, whether you can carry two crews through winter, whether it is time to hire. That is the thinking this covers.</p>
<p class="p" style="margin-bottom:0">It is not a full-time hire. Most firms this size need the hours, not a title. Call it a CFO on retainer, reviewing decisions before they happen.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</section>

<section style="padding:var(--pad-major) 0">
<div class="seg-in c-center">
<p class="eyebrow"><span class="mark"></span>From clients</p>
<div class="quotes">
<blockquote class="quote quote-card">
<p class="quote-t ph">Kevin had the AP off my desk inside a week. I stopped checking my email for supplier calls.</p>
<cite class="quote-w ph">First name L.<span class="quote-r">Owner, a subcontracting firm, Vancouver Island</span></cite>
</blockquote>
<blockquote class="quote quote-card">
<p class="quote-t ph">He asked about our holdback schedule on the first call. Nobody else had asked in years.</p>
<cite class="quote-w ph">First name L.<span class="quote-r">Owner, a general contractor, Vancouver Island</span></cite>
</blockquote>
</div>
</div>
</section>

<section class="on-tan" style="padding:var(--pad-major) 0">
<div class="seg-in c-center">
<p class="eyebrow"><span class="mark"></span>Getting started</p>
<h2 class="h2">Four steps, and the first one is a phone call.</h2>
<ol class="steps">
<li class="step"><span class="pt-n">01</span><p>A call. Twenty minutes, and you do most of the talking.</p></li>
<li class="step"><span class="pt-n">02</span><p>We look at what you have now, in whatever state it is in.</p></li>
<li class="step"><span class="pt-n">03</span><p>A scope and a price, in writing, before anything moves.</p></li>
<li class="step"><span class="pt-n">04</span><p>Handover, once. Then it is off your plate for good.</p></li>
</ol>
<p class="small muted" style="margin:0 auto">Rather talk first? Call <a class="tel" href={site.tel}>{site.phone}</a> and skip the form.</p>
</div>
</section>

<section style="padding:var(--pad-major) 0">
<div class="seg-in split">
<div>
<p class="eyebrow"><span class="mark"></span>Where we work</p>
<h2 class="h2" style="margin-bottom:var(--space-5)">Based in Victoria. Working across British Columbia.</h2>
<p class="p">Parallel is based in Victoria, but the work does not care where you are standing. Books get reconciled the same way in Victoria, Vancouver, Kelowna or Prince George.</p>
</div>
<div class="area-box">
<ul class="list-u">
<li>In Victoria, where Parallel is based.</li>
<li>Anywhere else in BC, delivered remotely, the same way.</li>
<li>One phone number, wherever the job is.</li>
</ul>
</div>
</div>
</section>

<div class="cta on-dark cta-c">
<div class="cta-in">
<div>
<p class="eyebrow"><span class="mark"></span>No surprises</p>
<h2 class="h2 cta-h">A CPA who answers the phone himself.</h2>
<p class="cta-p">Kevin Loo is a CPA, CMA who has worked as a subcontractor and for them. Call and find out what your books actually need, not what a sales script says they need.</p>
</div>
<div class="cta-act">
<a class="tel" href={site.tel}>{site.phone}</a>
<a class="btn btn-1" href={u('/contact/')}>Book a call</a>
</div>
</div>
</div>

<section style="padding:var(--pad-major) 0">
<div class="seg-in c-center">
<p class="eyebrow"><span class="mark"></span>Service area</p>
<h2 class="h2" style="margin-bottom:var(--space-5)">Available across British Columbia.</h2>
<p class="p">Every service on this page is available anywhere in the province, delivered remotely from the Victoria office.</p>
<ul class="cities">
<li><a class="city city-hq" href={u('/locations/victoria/')}>Victoria<span class="city-k">Head office</span></a></li>
<li><span class="city">Vancouver</span></li>
<li><span class="city">Surrey</span></li>
<li><span class="city">Burnaby</span></li>
<li><span class="city">Richmond</span></li>
<li><span class="city">Langley</span></li>
<li><span class="city">Abbotsford</span></li>
<li><span class="city">Kelowna</span></li>
<li><span class="city">Kamloops</span></li>
<li><span class="city">Nanaimo</span></li>
<li><span class="city">Courtenay</span></li>
<li><span class="city">Prince George</span></li>
</ul>
<p class="small muted" style="margin:0 auto">Not on the list? Call <a class="tel" href={site.tel}>{site.phone}</a>. Anywhere in BC is handled the same way.</p>
</div>
</section>

<section class="on-tan" style="padding:var(--pad-major) 0">
<div class="seg-in split">
<div>
<p class="eyebrow"><span class="mark"></span>Questions</p>
<h2 class="h2">What owners ask before the first call.</h2>
</div>
<ul class="faq">
<li class="faq-i">
<button class="faq-q" type="button" aria-expanded="true">What happens when I call?<span class="faq-s" aria-hidden="true">&ndash;</span></button>
<p class="faq-a">You talk to Kevin. He asks what the books look like now and what is eating your week. That is the whole first call.</p>
</li>
<li class="faq-i">
<button class="faq-q" type="button" aria-expanded="false">Can I get just one service, not all six?<span class="faq-s" aria-hidden="true">+</span></button>
<p class="faq-a faq-shut">Yes. Most clients start with one, usually bookkeeping or accounts payable, and add on later if it makes sense.</p>
</li>
<li class="faq-i">
<button class="faq-q" type="button" aria-expanded="false">What do I need to have ready?<span class="faq-s" aria-hidden="true">+</span></button>
<p class="faq-a faq-shut">Nothing. If you have last year's file and access to the bank feed, we move faster, but it is not a requirement.</p>
</li>
<li class="faq-i">
<button class="faq-q" type="button" aria-expanded="false">How is this different from an app?<span class="faq-s" aria-hidden="true">+</span></button>
<p class="faq-a faq-shut">An app gives you a login. This gives you a person who already knows what a holdback is and calls you back.</p>
</li>
<li class="faq-i">
<button class="faq-q" type="button" aria-expanded="false">Do you work with businesses outside Victoria?<span class="faq-s" aria-hidden="true">+</span></button>
<p class="faq-a faq-shut">Yes. Parallel works with contractors across British Columbia. The work is delivered remotely, so where you are does not change how it runs.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>

<section style="padding:var(--pad-major) 0">
<div class="seg-in c-center contact-c">
<p class="eyebrow"><span class="mark"></span>Get in touch</p>
<h2 class="h2" style="margin-bottom:var(--space-8);max-width:var(--measure)">Talk to Kevin, or send the short version.</h2>
<div class="ct-grid">
<div class="ct-card" style="background:var(--color-surface-alt)">
<div class="nap-body">
<p class="eyebrow" style="margin-bottom:var(--space-4)"><span class="mark"></span>Find us</p>
<a class="tel-lg nap-tel" href={site.tel}>{site.phone}</a>
<ul class="rows">
<li class="row"><span class="row-k">Email</span><span class="row-v"><a href={site.mailto}>{site.email}</a></span></li>
<li class="row"><span class="row-k">Address</span><span class="row-v">{site.address}</span></li>
<li class="row"><span class="row-k">Hours</span><span class="row-v ph">Monday&ndash;Friday, 8:30am&ndash;5:00pm PT</span></li>
</ul>
<p class="btn-row" style="margin-top:var(--space-6)"><a class="btn-txt" href={site.directions} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Get directions</a></p>
</div>
<div class="nap-map ct-map has-map"><iframe src={site.mapEmbed} title="Map showing the Parallel Accounting office" loading="lazy" referrerpolicy="no-referrer-when-downgrade"></iframe></div>
</div>
<form class="form ct-card" data-form method="post" action={site.formEndpoint} style="background:var(--color-surface-alt)">
<FormMeta source="Services page" />
<p class="eyebrow" style="margin-bottom:var(--space-4)"><span class="mark"></span>Send the short version</p>
<div class="form-row">
<label class="field field-s"><span class="field-lab">Name</span><input class="field-in" name="name" required type="text"></label>
<label class="field field-s"><span class="field-lab">Phone</span><input class="field-in" name="phone" type="tel"></label>
</div>
<label class="field field-w"><span class="field-lab">Email</span><input class="field-in" name="email" required type="email"></label>
<label class="field field-w"><span class="field-lab">What is going on with your books?</span><textarea class="field-in field-ta" name="message" required></textarea></label>
<button class="btn btn-2" type="submit">Send it over</button>
<p class="form-note" data-form-note>Kevin reads these himself. Expect a reply within one business day.</p>
</form>
</div>
</div>
</section>
